<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joanna II of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1373–<a href="/years/1435">1435</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joanna II (1373–1435) was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1414 to 1435, succeeding her brother Ladislaus as the last ruler of the Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a> line. Her reign was dominated by favourites and shifting alliances, and she adopted in turn Alfonso V of Aragon and Louis III of Anjou as heirs. Her death without children opened a war of succession between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a> and Alfonso V.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joanna-ii-of-naples.jpg" alt="Joanna II of Naples" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franciscus3008 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanna_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joanna was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zara</a>, present-day Zadar, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-naples">Charles III of Naples</a> and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durazzo</a>. In the autumn of 1401 Joanna married her first husband, William, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. They had no children, and William died in 1406. Joanna then took a lover, Pandolfello Piscopo, called Alopo, whom she appointed Grand Chamberlain, the first of several powerful favourites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1414 Joanna succeeded her brother Ladislaus. In early 1415 she was engaged to John, a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand I of Aragon</a>, but the betrothal was annulled, and on 10 August 1415 she married James II, Count of La Marche, to gain French support. When he did not receive the promised title of Prince of Taranto, he had Alopo killed, forced Joanna to name him King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and imprisoned her in her apartments, from which the nobles released her. A riot in 1416 forced him to send back his French administrators and renounce his title, and he later left Naples for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Meanwhile Joanna had begun her relationship with Sergianni Caracciolo, who later gained overwhelming power over the court.</p><p>She was crowned Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 28 October 1419, but her relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> worsened, and he called in Louis III of Anjou, who invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> in 1420. Joanna turned to King Alfonso V of Aragon, promising him the hereditary title to Naples, and he entered Naples in July 1421. After Alfonso had Caracciolo arrested in May 1423 and besieged the Castel Capuano, Joanna declared the adoption null and void and named Louis her heir. Alfonso returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In August 1432 Caracciolo, who had become ruler in all but name, was stabbed in his room in the Castel Capuano, after Joanna had plotted his assassination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The last years of the reign were relatively peaceful. Louis took up residence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> but died in 1434, and Joanna designated his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a> as heir. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 2 February 1435 and was buried in the Church of the Santissima Annunziata. With her death the senior line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a> became extinct, and she was succeeded by René. Her reputation for scandalous relationships gave rise in popular tradition to lurid tales, among them a legend of a trapdoor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a> through which used lovers were thrown to sea monsters, later a crocodile, though the legend is likely fictional.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Joanna II of Naples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.11,15.23&amp;year=1413" aria-label="Zadar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4915 602 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Joanna II of Naples"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5365 872h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5365 872h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.11,15.23&amp;year=1413">What was here in 1413? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
